data.content:
**What happened:** C# 14's new span conversion rules cause `array.Contains(num)` to bind to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of the old `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. Expression trees cannot represent `Span<T>`-based calls, so this throws at runtime (or compile time in some contexts).

**How to fix** — force the call to resolve to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast inside the expression
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three prevent the compiler from picking the `Span<T>` overload, keeping the expression tree representable.
